// engine/level/bg.emp — Plane B (background) drawing routines (§2 A.5)
//
// T1 (zone-wide):     BG_Init loads act_bg_tiles into the shared BG VRAM region
//                     (slots 1024-1471, $8000-$B7FF — bounded above by the
//                     relocated SAT at $B800; see BG_TILE_REGION_BYTES below),
//                     then blits act_bg_layout into Plane B nametable. Both
//                     happen once at level load.
// T2/T3 (per-section): Section_RedrawPlanes (in section.emp) blits the section's
//                      sec_bg_layout at level init / cache recovery only.
//                      NULL sec_bg_layout = act-level T1 fallback. Teleports no
//                      longer redraw — a per-section BG swap at the seam needs
//                      the deferred mechanism in DEFERRED_WORK ("Per-section BG
//                      layout swap at the seam").
//
// Layout shape: each nametable layout is a raw 64x64 = 8192 bytes (full
// Plane B nametable — the BG is 512px tall for vertical parallax; the
// injector zero-pads 32-row layouts). Byte order is ROW-MAJOR:
// blob[(row*64 + col)*2] — each row's 64 columns are contiguous (row stride
// = 64 cols * 2 B = 128), which is BYTE-FOR-BYTE THE PLANE B NAMETABLE'S OWN
// VRAM ORDER. So a full-plane blit is one linear move.l run at autoinc $02
// (BG_Init below; Section_RedrawPlanes' Plane B half is the same run), and one
// plane ROW is a contiguous 64-word gather (Draw_BG_TileRow, engine/level/
// plane_buffer.emp). Both out-of-assembler writers now emit this order
// directly — tools/ojz_strip_gen.py always did, and tools/inject_editor_bg.py
// (which overwrites strip_gen's intermediate) stopped transposing.
//
// IT WAS COLUMN-MAJOR UNTIL REGIONS PART 2, STEP 2 (2026-09-15), and the flip
// is option R of that spec's §4.2. Column contiguity existed for one consumer,
// `Draw_BG_TileColumn`, which had ZERO callers for its whole life and is
// deleted in the same step; the column path returns in the big-levels parcel
// over a block map, not over this raw blob. What part 2 needs is the other
// axis: a background taller than the plane streams in by ROW, and the repaint
// at a region crossing sweeps row by row. Against a column-major blob a row
// producer is a 64-read strided gather at stride 128 — about twice the cost,
// on every streamed row, forever — while the two live blits had to be rewritten
// under part 2 regardless (after the plane shrink neither can drain a whole
// 8192-byte layout into a 4096-byte plane; each blits a WINDOW of
// PLANE_V_CELLS rows, which is a row loop by nature).
//
// ALL 64 ROWS ARE LIVE (NEW-5 close, 2026-08-05). Both runtime maintainers of
// Plane B cover the full plane — BG_Init and Section_RedrawPlanes each drain
// the whole BG_LAYOUT_SIZE image. The old 32-row maintainer limit silently
// half-reverted any BG art above row 31 on a redraw; that was NOT
// hypothetical: the shipped OJZ override carries real art in rows 32-63, and
// the full-plane vscroll wrap (v_factor_bg with a 512px plane) puts those
// rows on screen, including at spawn. The 512px vertical headroom the shape
// advertises is therefore real, in every writer.
// Tile-blob shape: 2-byte big-endian length header + raw deduped tile bytes
//   (mirrors S4LZ blob shape so engine can read length without a separate field).
// Decision rationale: docs/research/per-section-background.md (Q4 + Q5).

module engine.bg in bg

use engine.constants.{VRAM_SPRITE_TABLE, VRAM_PLANE_B_BYTES, BG_TILE_BASE_VRAM, BG_TILE_CAPACITY, VDP_DATA, VDP_CTRL, PLANE_H_CELLS, PLANE_V_CELLS}
use engine.structs.{Act}
use engine.vdp.{VdpTarget, VdpOp, vdp_comm}
use engine.z80_bus.{z80_stopped}

// `pub` because it is a CONTRACT, not an internal: the act's BG blob must be
// exactly this long, and games/sonic4/data/levels/ojz/act1/act_assets.emp now
// types its embed with it so a wrong-sized blob is an `array length mismatch` at
// build time rather than a transposed background at runtime (tools lens sweep D6).
pub const BG_LAYOUT_SIZE  = 64*64*2     // 8192 bytes (full Plane B nametable)
// The shared BG tile region runs from BG_TILE_BASE_VRAM up to the relocated
// sprite attr table at $B800 — so $8000..$B7FF = 448 tiles is the PHYSICAL run.
// It is no longer the clamp: the top of that run has been carved away by other
// regions, so the BG arena owns only BG_TILE_CAPACITY of it and a full 448-tile
// blob would spray over its neighbours' art rather than over the SAT. The clamp
// below uses the declared capacity; this constant stays because the `ensure`
// under it is what keeps the two facts from crossing.
//
// NO FIGURE IS STATED HERE ON PURPOSE. This comment twice said "400" and twice
// named a slot range, and both went stale under carves it never saw: the arena
// went 448 -> 400 (waterline strips) -> 388 -> 376 (the spring, in two edits),
// while the prose sat still. A fresh literal would stale on the identical clock.
// The live values are `BG_TILE_CAPACITY` itself (asserted against vram.toml by
// the game's own `ensure`) and the generated map, `docs/generated/vram-map-<game>.md`
// — read one of those, never a sentence.
const BG_TILE_REGION_BYTES = VRAM_SPRITE_TABLE - BG_TILE_BASE_VRAM   // $3800 = 14336 B = 448 tiles
const BG_TILE_CAPACITY_BYTES = BG_TILE_CAPACITY * 32                 // the arena's declared size in bytes; see above for why no total is written here
ensure(BG_TILE_CAPACITY_BYTES <= BG_TILE_REGION_BYTES,
       "games/<game>/vram.toml declares bg_region larger than the physical $8000..$B7FF run: BG_TILE_CAPACITY = {BG_TILE_CAPACITY} tiles = {BG_TILE_CAPACITY_BYTES} bytes against {BG_TILE_REGION_BYTES}. The BG arena is bounded above by the relocated sprite attribute table at VRAM_SPRITE_TABLE, so a capacity past it is not a bigger arena, it is a blob clamp that lets act art overwrite the SAT")

// -----------------------------------------------
// BG_Init — load shared BG tiles + blit Plane B nametable.
//
// In:  a0 = Act descriptor pointer (read only — preserved)
// Out: none
// Clobbers: d0, d1, a1, a2
//
// Display assumed off (called from Level_LoadArt, before display enable).
// Both writes are blocking via VDP DATA port; autoincrement = 2 bytes/word.
// The Z80 bus is held for the full copy brackets (~21ms + ~12ms worst case)
// — music already playing through level load takes an audible DAC gap
// (accepted at load; the VBlank flag-bracket idiom is the alternative if
// load-time audio continuity ever matters).
// -----------------------------------------------
pub proc BG_Init (a0: *Act) clobbers(d0-d1/a1-a2) preserves(a3/sr) {
        movem.l a3, -(sp)
        movea.l a0, a3                  // a3 = act ptr (preserve)
        // Mask interrupts for the blocking VDP copies: a VBlank mid-copy lets
        // the VInt handler repoint the VDP address and the remainder of the
        // blob sprays to wrong addresses (large BG blobs span >1 frame of
        // copy loop). Mirrors Section_RedrawPlanes' guard.
        move.w  sr, -(sp)
        move.w  #$2700, sr

        // --- load BG tile blob into shared region at BG_TILE_BASE_VRAM ---
        movea.l Act.act_bg_tiles(a3), a1
        cmpa.w  #0, a1
        beq     .skip_tiles
        move.w  (a1)+, d1               // d1.w = tile-bytes length (uncompressed)
        beq     .skip_tiles
        // Capacity guard (last line of defense): clamp the copy length to what the
        // BG arena is DECLARED to own — BG_TILE_CAPACITY tiles, not the physical
        // $8000..$B7FF run. Above the arena sit `waterline_strips` (48 tiles, item
        // 9d) and then the SAT at $B800, and a blob longer than the declared
        // capacity would overwrite the first before it ever reached the second.
        // The build tools should keep BG art within budget (inject_editor_bg.py
        // gates the final blob on the same constant); this is what guarantees no
        // corruption of either neighbour if a generator gate drifts.
        cmpi.w  #BG_TILE_CAPACITY_BYTES, d1
        bls     .bg_len_ok
        move.w  #BG_TILE_CAPACITY_BYTES, d1
    .bg_len_ok:
        // Tier-1 blit: copy the tile body a LONGWORD at a time (autoinc 2 keeps
        // it linear in VRAM). The count is quartered, so the length guard must be
        // RE-DERIVED — the parcel-6 word-count guard (lsr #1) does not survive the
        // move.l translation, it just underflows on a different set of lengths.
        //
        // The clamp above bounds the copy from ABOVE. It does NOT bound it from
        // below: a drifted length of 1..3 bytes passes the `beq` (nonzero) and the
        // `bls` (in range), then lsr.w #2 -> 0 and subq -> $FFFF, and dbf would run
        // 65,536 LONGWORDS = 256KB straight through the SAT and every other VRAM
        // structure. Close it by taking the LONGWORD count FIRST and skipping on
        // zero: lengths 1,2,3 all lsr#2 -> 0 -> `beq` skips BEFORE the subq, so the
        // underflow is unreachable. Lengths >= 4 copy floor(len/4) longwords and
        // drop the sub-longword tail (len 5 -> 1 longword + 1 dropped byte). Real
        // blobs are 32-byte granular, and BOTH producers now assert len%4==0 —
        // ojz_strip_gen.py (the unconditional emitter; assert added NEW-4
        // 2026-08-05 — the old comment here credited only the injector, which
        // runs solely when an editor override exists) and inject_editor_bg.py
        // — so the drop never fires in practice; it is the last line of defense.
        //
        // Both counter ops sit ABOVE the z80_stopped bracket on purpose:
        // .skip_tiles is past the bracket's release, so a guard branch taken from
        // inside it would leave the Z80 halted for the rest of the level — the
        // bracket refuses that shape outright (`[context.escape]`). Neither the
        // bus request nor the VDP setup touches d1, so the hoist is free. (No
        // assert on the drop: the debug expansion would push
        // .skip_tiles past short-branch reach and force shape-dependent width
        // pins — ledgered instead; the emitter always emits 32-byte multiples.)
        lsr.w   #2, d1                  // longword count = bytes/4
        beq     .skip_tiles             // 1..3-byte (sub-long) blob -> nothing to copy
        subq.w  #1, d1                  // dbf counter (safe: longword count >= 1)
        with z80_stopped {
            move.w  #$8F02, VDP_CTRL        // (VDP_CTRL).l — autoincrement = 2
            move.l  #vdp_comm(BG_TILE_BASE_VRAM, VdpTarget.Vram, VdpOp.Write), VDP_CTRL
            lea     VDP_DATA, a2            // (VDP_DATA).l
        .tile_copy:
            move.l  (a1)+, (a2)
            dbf     d1, .tile_copy
        }
    .skip_tiles:

        // --- blit BG nametable to Plane B ---
        movea.l Act.act_bg_layout(a3), a1
        cmpa.w  #0, a1
        beq     .skip_nt
        // Second consumer of the $2700 mask above. The mask is STILL load-bearing
        // after the row-major flip, and for the reason that outlived the
        // autoincrement excursion: the VInt handler repoints the VDP address
        // register, so a VBlank landing inside this 8192-byte blocking copy sends
        // the remainder of the image to whatever address the handler left behind.
        // (The excursion itself is gone — the blit now runs at the default autoinc
        // $02 from first word to last, so Flush_VDP_Shadow's every-VBlank reg $0F
        // re-blit can no longer contradict it. That removes one of the two hazards
        // the mask covers, not both.)
        if DEBUG == 1 {
            move.w  sr, d0
            andi.w  #$0700, d0
            assert.w d0, hs, #$0600     // IPL >= 6: no VBlank can land mid-copy
        }
        with z80_stopped {
            // ROW-MAJOR blit, and it is a straight linear copy: the layout blob's
            // byte order (blob[(row*64 + col)*2]) IS the Plane B nametable's VRAM
            // order, so the whole BG_LAYOUT_SIZE image drains as one autoinc-$02
            // move.l run with a single VDP command setup. The 64 per-column command
            // setups the column-major blit needed are gone with it.
            //
            // No length guard needed: the geometry is the compile-time constant
            // BG_LAYOUT_SIZE, never derived from blob data, so the sub-longword
            // underflow that bites the tile blit above cannot occur — the layout
            // blob is a fixed 8192 B image by contract (act_assets.emp types the
            // embed with BG_LAYOUT_SIZE, so a wrong-sized blob is a build error).
            //
            // The count is BG_LAYOUT_SIZE/4 - 1 = 2047, which is past a moveq's
            // signed byte, so it loads with a move.w. Do not "optimise" it back.
            move.w  #$8F02, VDP_CTRL       // (VDP_CTRL).l — autoincrement 2 (one cell)
            move.l  #vdp_comm(VRAM_PLANE_B_BYTES, VdpTarget.Vram, VdpOp.Write), VDP_CTRL
            lea     VDP_DATA, a2           // (VDP_DATA).l
            move.w  #BG_LAYOUT_SIZE/4 - 1, d0
        .nt_copy:
            move.l  (a1)+, (a2)
            dbf     d0, .nt_copy
        }
    .skip_nt:
        move.w  (sp)+, sr               // restore interrupt mask
        movem.l (sp)+, a3
        rts
}

// §4.2: BG_RedrawForSection deleted. Plane B is redrawn atomically alongside
// Plane A via Section_RedrawPlanes (see engine/level/section.emp), triggered
// by the Section_Plane_Dirty flag at level init and cache recovery only.
// Teleports no longer set it: they are pure coordinate rebases — world
// coordinates, plane mapping (mod 64) and scroll (mod 512) are all invariant
// under the $1000px shift, so a redraw would write byte-identical content
// (docs/research/teleport-rebase.md).

// ---- THE PLANE GEOMETRY FAMILY, pinned across ALL its members in this file (2026-09-11) ----
// The LS-8a plane-wrap pins at the end of engine/level/section.emp and plane_buffer.emp named
// this file as hand-spelling the same PLANE_H_CELLS x PLANE_V_CELLS geometry, unpinned. The
// sites were enumerated by what TOUCHES the plane size (every constant and instruction whose
// value must move if the plane does, read over the whole file), not by grepping a literal.
//
// THE TWO PER-AXIS PINS WERE DELETED ON 2026-09-15 (regions part 2, step 2) BECAUSE THEIR
// SITES WERE. They named three instructions, all of them in BG_Init's nametable blit and all
// of them artefacts of the column-major order: `move.w #$8F80` and `cmpi.w #64` (horizontal)
// and `moveq #32-1` (vertical). The row-major blit is one linear `move.l` run, so its three
// remaining literals are the autoincrement $02 (a CELL, not a plane), the plane's VRAM base
// (a layout constant, not a size) and `BG_LAYOUT_SIZE/4 - 1`, which FOLLOWS the constant and
// so needs no pin at all. Deleting them is not a loosening: an `ensure` whose enumerated
// sites no longer exist cannot fail on the change it names, and leaving it would have read as
// coverage this file no longer needs (docs/DEFERRED_WORK's vacuous-gate rule).
//
// BG_LAYOUT_SIZE keeps its OWN pin, and it is the only one left here: it depends on both axes
// and is still hand-spelled. It was already separate for this reason — folded into the axis
// pins, a vertical-only move fired the HORIZONTAL message too and blamed an axis that had not
// moved (measured 2026-09-11, M25/M26).
ensure(BG_LAYOUT_SIZE == PLANE_H_CELLS * PLANE_V_CELLS * 2,
       "BG LAYOUT SIZE (engine/level/bg.emp, 2026-09-11). engine/level/bg.emp's BG_LAYOUT_SIZE no longer matches the plane: it is {BG_LAYOUT_SIZE} bytes, and a full Plane B nametable is PLANE_H_CELLS x PLANE_V_CELLS x 2 = {PLANE_H_CELLS} x {PLANE_V_CELLS} x 2 = {PLANE_H_CELLS * PLANE_V_CELLS * 2}. THE SITE: `pub const BG_LAYOUT_SIZE = 64*64*2`, which BG_Init's nametable blit drains whole, Section_RedrawPlanes' plane-B blit and Draw_BG_TileColumn read column by column, and games/sonic4/data/levels/ojz/act1/act_assets.emp uses as the embed type of OJZ_Act1_BG_Layout. WHAT THIS PIN DOES NOT COVER: the per-axis literals in BG_Init (the PLANE_H_CELLS and PLANE_V_CELLS pins above, one of which fires beside this one whenever a plane size moves); the layout's column-major ORDER, which no size can express; and the out-of-assembler writers of the blob (tools/inject_editor_bg.py, tools/ojz_strip_gen.py), which this pin cannot see")
